WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.170
The moment we wake up early morning and start
with our daily chores, we

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:10.660
depend on various items.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.860
You may say there are many in the list!

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:14.860
Yes!

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.710
But most
of the items used by us are made up of a single

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:21.130
material collectively known as
PLASTIC!

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:22.430
Yes!

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:27.619
The tooth brush handle, the comb, plates,
bottles, lunch boxes, bodies of

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:33.910
refrigerators, water purifiers, laptops and
so many other articles!

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.480
The list
would be endless!

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.720
So are all these articles made up of plastic?

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:40.720
Yes!

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.250
But they are quite different
from each other.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.820
So how is that possible?

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:50.390
Well, it is possible because plastic
itself comes in various forms.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.320
What do we mean by this?

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.350
To understand this, let us draw two figures.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.930
Can you tell me the difference between the
two?

00:00:58.930 --> 00:00:59.930
Yes!

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.879
The first one is linear in
fashion.

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:06.540
On the other hand, the second one is inter-linked.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.680
This type is called
the cross linking type.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.570
But why are we studying these now?

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:15.100
We know that plastic is a polymer.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:19.619
Now the various monomeric units in the
different plastics are arranged in different

00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:21.420
patterns.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:22.420
And what will these

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:23.420
patterns be?

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:24.420
Yes!

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.469
They are either linearly arranged or they
are arranged in a

00:01:27.469 --> 00:01:29.560
cross linked manner.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.329
Not only the arrangement but even the difference
in

00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:36.649
monomeric units that make up the polymer is
responsible for giving us the

00:01:36.649 --> 00:01:38.579
various forms.

00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:42.859
However, plastics are usually classified in
two major categories.

00:01:42.859 --> 00:01:46.509
Let us take two
commonly used examples.

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:49.560
One is the handle of our frying pan while
the other

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.900
is a simple water bottle.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.189
What will happen when you heat the two?

00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:59.079
Needless to say the bottle will melt within
no time, but the handle will just

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:01.399
heat up without much physical change.

00:02:01.399 --> 00:02:03.560
Can you tell me why?

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:07.289
If both are
plastics, then why is there so much difference?

00:02:07.289 --> 00:02:11.810
Well that is because the two are different
TYPES of plastics!

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.720
The first one which
makes up the handle is called the THERMOSETTING

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:16.720
plastic.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.560
While the other
type is referred to as THERMOPLASTIC.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.390
Looking at the incidence of heating can you
tell me the basic difference

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.420
between the two?

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:27.920
Yes!

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.080
Thermosetting plastics are those which DO
NOT MELT on heating.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:33.890
While
manufacturing the particular article, when

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:40.000
the plastic is casted into mould, it
takes up the form permanently.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.000
It cannot be remoulded later.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.820
The two most commonly used examples of thermosetting
plastics are

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.920
Melamine and Bakelite.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.750
And where are these used?

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:55.150
Melamine is known to withstand high amounts
of heat.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:03:00.070
It is therefore used in
making tiles, floorings and kitchenware.

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:04.570
Bakelite on the other hand is used in making
handles of kitchen utensils and

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:09.240
switches as it is poor conductor of heat and
electricity.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.370
So this was about thermosetting plastics.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:15.280
What could be the properties of Thermoplastics
then?

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.980
In contrast to the
thermosetting type, these can be moulded or

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:22.440
simply melted on heating.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:23.580
Yes!

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:28.120
Thermoplastics can be easily bent, or heated
to remould it into various

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.650
forms.

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:32.850
And do we know any such type used daily?

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:34.410
Absolutely!

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.270
Polythene and
PVC are the most common ones.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.460
Where do we use Polythene?

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:41.460
Yes!

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:47.600
Right from bags to the adhesive tapes,
polythene is used in various forms.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.680
And what is PVC?

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.340
It stands for Polyvinyl chloride.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:55.540
PVC comes in two distinct
forms.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:00.780
The first one is hard or rigid PVC that is
used in making strong structures

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:03.120
like construction pipes.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.010
In contrast, the other type is soft PVC which
is used for

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.040
making containers and bottles used daily by
us.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:15.950
These were a few examples explaining how types
of plastics are used.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.900
But can
you tell me why plastic is a choice against

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.430
so many other naturally occurring
materials?

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:26.360
What properties of Plastics make it a preferred
material over others

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.020
in the list?

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:31.580
Well in order to know this you will have to
watch our next video!

